Session 2 Reflection

Management of AI Products
Personal Learning Journal – Session 2

The second session was much more practical because it focused on the product lifecycle and how products evolve through continuous learning rather than following 
a fixed process.

One idea that particularly hit with me was that the product lifecycle is a managed learning loop. I have often treated software development as completing a 
list of requirements and moving to the next project. This session reminded me that products continuously evolve based on customer learning. 

This is especially relevant for AI products because user expectations and AI capabilities change rapidly.

The discussion about decision gates made me reflect on my own projects. I usually try to finalize many decisions early, believing it helps avoid future changes. 
However, the professor explained that decisions should be deferred whenever possible unless they affect the product architecture or strategic direction. 
The example of choosing the paint color only after building the house foundation made this concept very easy to understand. 
I think I sometimes spend unnecessary time discussing UI details when the actual business workflow itself is still evolving.

Another learning was the distinction between a project and a product. A project solves one customer's problem, whereas a product is expected to solve similar 
problems for many unknown customers. This made me rethink PrepLens AI. Initially, I was building it based on my own interview experiences and a few colleagues' 
feedback. Going forward, I should validate assumptions with a wider audience instead of assuming all job seekers have similar needs.

The professor also emphasized documenting decisions and maintaining evidence. At first, I thought documentation was mainly for compliance. After this discussion, 
I realized that documenting why decisions were made becomes extremely valuable when the product evolves or when new team members join. 
For PrepLens AI(My product idea), I have changed pricing, AI prompts, and feature priorities several times. If I had documented those decisions properly,
I could now better evaluate whether those changes actually improved the product.

The conversation about AI Product Managers also made me reflect on my own preparation. I already understand AI technologies reasonably well because 
I work with Azure OpenAI and LLM-based automation. However, I now realize technical knowledge alone is not sufficient. An AI Product Manager must 
connect AI capabilities with business value, customer problems, risk, governance, and product outcomes. 
That is a much broader responsibility than simply selecting the right model.

One interesting point was the professor's advice to preserve learning material instead of trying to memorize everything.
I often feel pressure to remember every framework and every concept. Instead, I should focus on knowing where to find information and understanding how 
to apply it when required. That approach feels much more realistic.

Overall, this session helped me think beyond feature development. It encouraged me to become more deliberate in making product decisions, documenting reasoning, 
validating assumptions, and continuously learning from customers.

Key takeaway for myself:Successful AI products are not created by choosing the best AI model; they are created by continuously learning from users and making better product decisions.
